From: well0nez <well0nez@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] Revert the shared-memory registration of kernel callbacks

This is a prepared, not yet applied, surgical revert. It exists so that the
callback work can be undone without giving up anything else.

The change it reverts is right in its analysis: cpu_comm_register_callback()
only recorded the handler in a driver-private table and never entered the
routine in the shared-memory routine table, so the firmware -- which looks the
receiver up there -- aborted before putting anything on the wire
(cpu_comm_proto.c:230). Registering it the way IOCTL_INSTALL_RT does made the
handler visible, and dmesg confirmed it: "kernel handler registered for
comp=0x3e7fbc46".

It is reverted here because on the device it broke the capture driver's probe,
twice, in two different ways: THal_Vp_HDMI_SetPortMap timing out at -110 on one
cold start, and the EDID/HPD sequence failing with -EBUSY on the next. Two
different failures out of two boots means something nondeterministic, and a
half-understood fix on hardware is worse than the known gap it closes.

What this revert deliberately does NOT touch: sun50i-h713-afbd.c. The rule-1
corrections there (the wait_wce deadline moved to the measured bound, the
deadline turned into a real error path, the gamma expiry made audible) are
unrelated to this regression and are kept.

Signed-off-by: well0nez <well0nez@users.noreply.github.com>
---
--- a/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c	2026-09-07 10:08:41.081324743 +0200
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c	2026-09-07 10:08:41.082900780 +0200
@@ -1508,31 +1508,24 @@
 	return 0;
 }
 
-/*
- * The two routines the firmware calls back into. Registering them is not
- * only a matter of this driver: cpu_comm_register_callback() installs a
- * descriptor for the name in the routine table in shared memory, and
- * without that descriptor the firmware's own name lookup fails and it
- * never sends the call. So this has to happen before the bring-up
- * sequence arms the callbacks, and its failure is a probe failure.
- */
-#define H713_HDMIRX_CB_SIGNAL	"MipsHalCallback_SignalChange"
-#define H713_HDMIRX_CB_HOTPLUG	"MipsHalCallback_HdmiHotPlugByPortHandler"
-
 static int h713_hdmirx_register_callbacks(struct h713_hdmirx *rx)
 {
+	u32 sig = cpu_comm_name2id("MipsHalCallback_SignalChange",
+				   CPU_COMM_CPU_ARM);
+	u32 hpd = cpu_comm_name2id("MipsHalCallback_HdmiHotPlugByPortHandler",
+				   CPU_COMM_CPU_ARM);
 	int ret;
 
-	ret = cpu_comm_register_callback(H713_HDMIRX_CB_SIGNAL,
-					 h713_hdmirx_signal_cb, rx);
+	if (!sig || !hpd)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	ret = cpu_comm_register_callback(sig, h713_hdmirx_signal_cb, rx);
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
 
-	ret = cpu_comm_register_callback(H713_HDMIRX_CB_HOTPLUG,
-					 h713_hdmirx_hotplug_cb, rx);
+	ret = cpu_comm_register_callback(hpd, h713_hdmirx_hotplug_cb, rx);
 	if (ret) {
-		cpu_comm_unregister_callback(H713_HDMIRX_CB_SIGNAL,
-					     h713_hdmirx_signal_cb);
+		cpu_comm_unregister_callback(sig, h713_hdmirx_signal_cb);
 		return ret;
 	}
 
@@ -1541,9 +1534,11 @@
 
 static void h713_hdmirx_unregister_callbacks(struct h713_hdmirx *rx)
 {
-	cpu_comm_unregister_callback(H713_HDMIRX_CB_SIGNAL,
+	cpu_comm_unregister_callback(cpu_comm_name2id("MipsHalCallback_SignalChange",
+						      CPU_COMM_CPU_ARM),
 				     h713_hdmirx_signal_cb);
-	cpu_comm_unregister_callback(H713_HDMIRX_CB_HOTPLUG,
+	cpu_comm_unregister_callback(cpu_comm_name2id("MipsHalCallback_HdmiHotPlugByPortHandler",
+						      CPU_COMM_CPU_ARM),
 				     h713_hdmirx_hotplug_cb);
 }
 
@@ -1615,18 +1610,9 @@
 				     "cpu_comm-Shmem-Fenster nicht abbildbar\n");
 
 	ret = h713_hdmirx_register_callbacks(rx);
-	if (ret) {
-		/*
-		 * Registration now writes into the cpu_comm shared region, so
-		 * it can fail with -ENODEV for the same reason the bring-up
-		 * sequence below can: nothing orders this probe against the
-		 * cpu_comm driver's. Come back rather than give up.
-		 */
-		if (ret == -ENODEV)
-			ret = -EPROBE_DEFER;
+	if (ret)
 		return dev_err_probe(dev, ret,
 				     "Callbacks nicht registrierbar\n");
-	}
 
 	/* Stage 3: the firmware bring-up sequence. */
 	ret = h713_hdmirx_run_init_seq(rx);
diff -ruN a/drivers/soc/sunxi/cpu_comm/cpu_comm_api.c b/drivers/soc/sunxi/cpu_comm/cpu_comm_api.c
--- a/drivers/soc/sunxi/cpu_comm/cpu_comm_api.c	2026-09-07 10:08:41.078324733 +0200
+++ b/drivers/soc/sunxi/cpu_comm/cpu_comm_api.c	2026-09-07 10:08:41.080101739 +0200
@@ -22,36 +22,17 @@
  * open /dev/cpu_comm fd first, then the kernel handler. Both see the same
  * 104-byte message; neither can inhibit the other or the acknowledgement
  * that follows. That hook point is before the split between the FIFO path
- * (channel field <= 4) and the workqueue path (> 4), so a kernel handler
- * sees both classes exactly once. Which class a given callback belongs to
- * is not a thing to rely on: every MipsHalCallback_SignalChange and
- * MipsHalCallback_HdmiHotPlugByPortHandler captured on the bench so far
- * arrived with channel field 0, the FIFO path, contrary to what doku/72
- * assumed about SignalChange.
- *
- * That hook point being independent of the routine table is what made an
- * earlier version of this file believe the table did not matter. It does,
- * on the other side of the link: registering a handler here does not make
- * the co-processor send anything. The firmware resolves MipsHalCallback_*
- * through the routine table in shared memory -- SendComm2CPUEx() calls
- * FindRoutine() and returns -3 when the id is not in the table, before it
- * touches the message FIFO -- so a callback with no descriptor is never
- * put on the wire, and the hook never runs. Every incoming call captured
- * on the bench proves the lookup happened: the message carries the
- * descriptor's owner field back at +0x10, copied from routine_find_buf+4
- * by SendComm2CPUEx() step 14, and that field only exists in the table.
- * cpu_comm_register_callback() therefore installs the descriptor as
- * IOCTL_INSTALL_RT does, and takes the routine's name to build it.
+ * (channel field <= 4, e.g. HdmiHotPlugByPortHandler) and the workqueue
+ * path (> 4, e.g. SignalChange), so a kernel handler sees both classes
+ * exactly once.
  */
 
